Apple's former chairman Arthur Levinson plans to sell 90,000 Apple shares worth approximately &#36;31.8 million, according to a <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/320193/000192109426001188/xsl144X01/primary_doc.xml">new filing</a> with the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2022/01/Apple-Logo-Cash-Feature-Mint.jpg" alt="" width="2676" height="1504" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-834995" /><br/>
The shares are held in Levinson's trust and were originally acquired in February 2001, more than 25 years ago. The filing lists November 16 as the expected sale date, but does not confirm whether the transaction was completed.<br/>
<br/>
Apple stock traded at around 35 cents per share on a split-adjusted basis at the time. At that price, the shares being offered for sale would have cost approximately &#36;31,500, compared with their value of nearly &#36;32 million in the filing, which is roughly a thousandfold increase. <br/>
<br/>
The proposed sale comes shortly after a change in Levinson's role at Apple. He joined the board in 2000 and served as chairman from 2011 until September, when <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/tim-cook/">Tim Cook</a> became executive chairman and John Ternus <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/04/20/tim-cook-stepping-down/">took over as CEO</a>. Levinson remains on the board as lead independent director.<br/>
<br/>
